{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And Moses saw the people, that it was exposed, for Aaron had exposed them to shame among all who rose up against them.</b> Because the Sages said (Yoma 86a): one publicizes the flatterers because of the desecration of HaShem's name — for HaShem, blessed be He, examines all hidden things and punishes the sinner for everything concealed, and since he is presumed righteous in the eyes of creatures, they will cast aspersion on the attribute of justice; therefore it is a commandment to publicize all their deeds, to reveal their wickedness in the congregation. And for this reason Aaron brought their evil thought out into the open. And this is what is said: ״אַתָּה יָדַעְתָּ אֶת הָעָם כִּי בְרָע הוּא״ — meaning, you know through the holy spirit that rests upon you that they are always thinking evil; therefore I said that it is good to expose the sinners, so that they become a שִׁמְצָה (whisper/reproach) among those who rise up against them, to publicize their disgrace in public. And this is the view of the Midrash that says they were stricken with tzara'at, as it says ״כִּי פָרֻעַ הוּא״, and it is written of the metzora (Leviticus 13:45) ״רֹאשׁוֹ יִהְיֶה פָרוּעַ״ — as deduced in the Yalkut (and in Tanhuma, Tazria 4) — for tzara'at too, its purpose is to bring out into the light the inner evil that is in the sinner's heart, for this reason the metzora is called by notarikon מוֹצִיא רָע (one who brings out evil), as deduced in the Yalkut, parashat Metzora (remedied 554), in the matter of the peddler. Even though there the deduction of מוֹצִיא רָע applies to the talebearer of evil tongue, nevertheless it appears to me that it means one who brings to light every inner evil. And therefore tzara'at was hinted at here by the word פָּרֻעַ, whose meaning is ״uncovered/revealed״ — so too tzara'at reveals itself upon sinners. And in Song of Songs (1:12) it is written ״נִרְדִּי נָתַן רֵיחוֹ״, and the Targum explains this: ״ונחית עליהון מכתש סגירא״ — for the verse speaks of how they became a שִׁמְצָה among those who rose up against them, and their odor became foul in the eyes of all the nations that rose up against them, and this is ״נָתַן רֵיחוֹ״ — that is, to others he gave off the odor that had been hidden within him; and when it was revealed, then they became a שִׁמְצָה among those who rose up against them, for they found an opening to revile Israel for what they did against Elohim and against Moses. And from this inference you learn that this matter does not exist among the nations." "And this is clarified along the lines of what we find in Ruth Rabbah, parashat Vayeshev (88a): ״מִכָּל פְּשָׁעַי הַצִּילֵנִי חֶרְפַּת נָבָל אַל תְּשִׂימֵנִי״ (Psalms 39:9). Rabbi Hama bar Hanina said: the nations of the world were not worthy of having among them those afflicted with דּוֹוִים and סְקוּפִים (wasting diseases and scabs), but they have them only so that they will not point the finger at Israel and say to them, 'You are a nation of the diseased and scabbed' — hence ״חֶרְפַּת נָבָל אַל תְּשִׂימֵנִי״. And Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachman said: the nations of the world were not worthy of having among them בַּעֲלֵי חֲטָטִין (those afflicted with maladies) — and why do they have them? So that they will not point the finger at Israel and say, 'You are not a nation of מְצֹרָעִים (metzoraim)' — hence ״חֶרְפַּת נָבָל אַל תְּשִׂימֵנִי״. And one may probe: about what do they disagree, and what is the difference between the דּוֹוִים and the מְצֹרָעִים? And it appears to me, as deduced in the Midrash: they sinned with ״this״ — ״כִּי זֶה מֹשֶׁה הָאִישׁ״ — and they were stricken with ״this״, as it says (Lamentations 5:17) ״עַל זֶה הָיָה דָוֶה לִבֵּנוּ״, and they were appeased with ״this״, as it says (Exodus 35:4–5) ״זֶה הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳, קְחוּ מֵאִתְּכֶם תְּרוּמָה לַה׳״. They sinned with ״these״ — ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — and they were stricken with ״these״, as it says (Lamentations 5:17) ״עַל אֵלֶּה חָשְׁכוּ עֵינֵינוּ״, and they were appeased with ״these״, as it says (Exodus 38:21) ״אֵלֶּה פְקוּדֵי הַמִּשְׁכָּן״. You see from this Midrash that there were two kinds of sin here: the first is what they sinned against HaShem, blessed be He, saying ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל״, and for this they were stricken with tzara'at, which brings to light every evil thought. And they had already sinned in thought, as it says ״אַתָּה יָדַעְתָּ אֶת הָעָם כִּי בְרָע הוּא״ as explained above, and the tzara'at would bring their evil out to the outside to publicize it upon them. And since the law of every metzora is that his dwelling is outside the camp, and here all Israel were metzoraim — how could they go outside the camp? Therefore it says ״וּמֹשֶׁה יִקַּח אֶת הָאֹהֶל וְנָטָה לוֹ מִחוּץ לַמַּחֲנֶה הַרְחֵק מִן הַמַּחֲנֶה וְקָרָא לוֹ אֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״ — as if there were the camp of the Shekhinah and they were distanced from the camp of the Shekhinah. And as for what is said ״עַל אֵלֶּה חָשְׁכוּ עֵינֵינוּ״ — the word ״זֶה״ indicates something a person sees with his eyes and points at with his finger, saying 'this is it'; and the word ״אֵלֶּה״ indicates something one grasps through inner vision even though he does not see it with his eyes. Therefore it says they sinned with ״these״ concerning the sin they committed against Elohim — for man shall not see Him and live — and it says they sinned with ״this״ concerning what they sinned against Moses, acting against the sense of sight, for with their own eyes they saw the uprightness of his way and even so harbored suspicions against him. And according to this line of reasoning, it is difficult what it says: ״עַל זֶה הָיָה דָוֶה לִבֵּנוּ עַל אֵלֶּה חָשְׁכוּ עֵינֵינוּ״ — it should have been stated the opposite." "And the explanation of the matter is that one is always stricken through the very thing that was the cause of the transgression. For in what they sinned against Elohim, the sin was drawn from the eye to the heart — for the eye sees and afterward the heart covets. And we have already said that because of the abundance of gold that HaShem showered upon them, they chose the form of an ox, for from there comes the source of gold, as explained above regarding the verse (Exodus 30:13) ״זֶה יִתְּנוּ כָּל הָעוֹבֵר עַל הַפְּקוּדִים״ etc. And therefore it says ״עַל אֵלֶּה חָשְׁכוּ עֵינֵינוּ״ — for the eye caused them this sin. But in what they sinned against Moses, the sin was drawn from the heart to the eye — for because they were constantly harboring thoughts in their heart and were jealous of his virtue and suspected him, they became blind even in the sense of sight and denied things they had seen with their own eyes; for this is the nature of jealousy, through which one becomes blind, acting contrary to what the sense testifies. Therefore it says ״עַל זֶה הָיָה דָוֶה לִבֵּנוּ״ — for the heart was the cause for them of acting against the very thing indicated by the sense of sight, hinted at by ״this.״ And from here they saw fit to say that all wasting diseases and scabs that come upon Israel — all of it is because of the sin of casting words against the leaders of every generation. And the tzara'at comes on account of the sin of idolatry, which is drawn from the lust for money. And the darkening of the eye is measure for measure, because ״the human eye is never sated״ (Ecclesiastes 4:8); therefore they are stricken in the eye. And according to our way we may say that the darkening of the eye is the tzara'at that darkens a person's eye, as is known from natural causes. And these two evil traits are not found so much among the nations, as deduced in tractate Sanhedrin (39b): it is written (Ezekiel 11:12) ״וּכְמִשְׁפְּטֵי הַגּוֹיִם עֲשִׂיתֶם״, and it is written ״וּכְמִשְׁפְּטֵי הַגּוֹיִם לֹא עֲשִׂיתֶם״ (ibid. 5:7). How so? The corrupt among them — you did as they do; the upright among them — you did not do as they do. For we find that they are upright in these two matters: both in the matter of divinity, as it is written (Jeremiah 2:11) ״Has a nation exchanged its gods? And they are not gods at all! But My people has exchanged its glory for that which does not profit״; and in the matter of leaders, that they do not kick against them, as we find with the people of Nineveh, who listened even to the prophets of Israel." "Therefore Rabbi Hama bar Hanina said: the nations of the world were not worthy of having among them those afflicted with wasting diseases and scabs — and that is all illness and every blow that comes upon those who kick against their leaders, as it says ״עַל זֶה הָיָה דָוֶה לִבֵּנוּ״. And his view is that what is said, ״כִּי פְרָעֹה אַהֲרֹן לְשִׁמְצָה בְּקָמֵיהֶם״, refers to the nations pointing the finger at Israel for what they did against their leaders." "And Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachman says: the nations of the world were not worthy of having among them those afflicted with maladies — and this is a species of the kinds of spreading tzara'at that bloom on the body, in which there bloomed heresy and denial of divinity — but it is only so that they not point the finger at Israel and say, 'You are not a nation of metzoraim.' And he holds that what is said, ״כִּי פְרָעֹה אַהֲרֹן לְשִׁמְצָה בְּקָמֵיהֶם״, means that an enemy would revile Israel on account of the plague and the tzara'at that bloomed upon them for saying ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל״ on account of the abundance of gold that HaShem showered upon them. And his view is that regarding this David said: ״מִכָּל פְּשָׁעַי הַצִּילֵנִי, חֶרְפַּת נָבָל אַל תְּשִׂימֵנִי״ — for an enemy adversary would revile, saying that I am a נָבָל (vile wretch) who vilifies the Rock of his salvation on account of the lust for gold and wealth, as it says of him (Jeremiah 17:11) ״וּבְאַחֲרִיתוֹ יִהְיֶה נָבָל״ — for every lover of silver is a miserly man like Naval the Carmelite, whose name was Naval and vile conduct was with him, and as it says (Isaiah 32:5) ״לֹא יִקָּרֵא עוֹד לְנָבָל נָדִיב״ — implying that they are opposites. And this will be explained further, with HaShem's help, in parashat Ha'azinu on the verse ״עַם נָבָל וְלֹא חָכָם״. And concerning this David sought and said: ״חֶרְפַּת נָבָל אַל תְּשִׂימֵנִי״. And even though even if the enemy reviles me and if I am judged I have an answer and a defense, nevertheless ״נֶאֱלַמְתִּי לֹא אֶפְתַּח פִּי״ — I am not permitted to speak my defense — ״כִּי אַתָּה עָשִׂיתָ״: You caused Israel to sin, on account of the abundance of silver and gold that You showered upon them, and this defense is not to be spoken before the gentile. Therefore ״הָסֵר מֵעָלַי נִגְעֶךָ״ — this is the plague of tzara'at that bloomed upon Israel — ״מִתִּגְרַת יָדְךָ אֲנִי כָלִיתִי״. מִתִּגְרַת is an expression of outpouring and flow, and it means: because from Your hand there flowed to me an abundance of silver and gold, therefore ״אֲנִי כָלִיתִי״ — I was destroyed. And he added the word ״אֲנִי כָלִיתִי״ to say that no evil descends from above and the Elohim intended it for good — rather, I brought destruction upon myself and corrupted my deeds on account of the abundance of silver and gold that was showered upon me. ״בְּתוֹכָחוֹת עַל עָוֹן יִסַּרְתָּ אִישׁ״ — תוֹכָחוֹת is an expression of וִכּוּחַ (argument/reproach), and it means: on account of the argument that the adversary has with me, pointing the finger and saying, 'You are a nation of metzoraim' — therefore ״יִסַּרְתָּ אִישׁ״ — even the nations, who are called ״בְּנֵי אִישׁ״, as it says (Psalms 49:3) ״גַּם בְּנֵי אָדָם גַּם בְּנֵי אִישׁ״, and our Sages of blessed memory said (Yalkut Shimoni, Psalms 49, remeze 797): ״בְּנֵי אָדָם״ — these are Israel; ״בְּנֵי אִישׁ״ — these are the children of Noah. ״וַתֶּמֶס כָּעָשׁ חֲמוּדוֹ״ — through the fact that there are among them too those afflicted with maladies, and then they will not attribute it to Israel's sin but will say ״אַךְ הֶבֶל כָּל אָדָם סֶלָה״ — for one fate comes to all. And according to the view of the one who says ״they were not worthy of having wasting diseases״ etc. — on account of their casting words against their leaders — he expounded ״חֶרְפַּת נָבָל״ like ״זְכֹר זֹאת אוֹיֵב חֵרֵף ה׳״ (Psalms 74:18) — to say that every mouth that speaks villainy, as we find that they cast words against Moses in several matters. And the discerning one will hear and add lesson."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {1 "The Kli Yakar ends comment [1] with a difficulty: if זֶה relates to sight and אֵלֶה to inner apprehension, then the verse should have said עַל זֶה חָשְׁכוּ עֵינֵינוּ and עַל אֵלֶּה הָיָה דָוֶה לִבֵּנוּ. He will resolve this in comment [2]."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:25:49.817539497Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}